<td id="S3.T1.3.1.5.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.5.4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Implicit</span></td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">No</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">No</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Adjacent Matrix</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Textual</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;">
<table id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GraphDPR</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.3.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite></td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;">
<table id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full,</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Redundant,</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Implicit</span></td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">No</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 1.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.6.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">No</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Summary of existing GTRs. ‘Charac.’ indicates the characteristics; ‘Enc.’ and ‘Train’ denote whether the GTR is generated by an external encoder and whether extra training is required, respectively. ‘Embed’ is short for ‘Embedding’.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As the table indicates, Embedding GTRs are generated by external encoders and typically require alignment training with the embedding space of VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. While the inevitable compression in embeddings leads to the loss of some topological information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, it also allows the learned embedding GTRs to focus on the most informative patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, both Visual GTRs and Textual GTRs convey the complete topology of a graph, enabling the graph to be uniquely reconstructed from these representations. However, textual GTRs, whether in the form of an Edge Set or an Adjacency List, present complex structures in a flattened, sequential context. This makes the topological information more implicit compared to the intuitive presentation offered by visual graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>. Moreover, the Adjacency Matrix often introduces redundant zero elements for non-existent edges, especially in the case of sparse graphs, making it rarely the primary choice for graph topology representation in existing works. However, the Adjacency Matrix can still be useful in specific scenarios where the explicit representation of connections is advantageous.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Constructing the <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R}_{ZS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R}_{ZS}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the variety of available GTRs, we carefully select the most suitable ones to form a dedicated set, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{ZS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>ZS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{ZS}}</annotation></semantics></math>, explicitly tailored to zero-shot graph QA scenarios. The selection follows three key principles:
(1) <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model-Agnostic</span>: The generation of GTRs must be decoupled from the VLM parameters, ensuring compatibility with closed-source VLMs. This is crucial as these models often offer superior performance but do not disclose model details. <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, embedding-based GTRs are excluded from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{ZS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ZS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{ZS}}</annotation></semantics></math> due to their reliance on embedding spaces, which are inaccessible given most leading models are closed-source</span>. (2) <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Variety</span>: The GTRs in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{ZS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{ZS}</annotation></semantics></math> should exhibit diversity to effectively address a wide range of question types. This diversity helps accommodate different types of graphs and QA tasks, utilizing formats like text and images that naturally align with VLM input formats. (3) <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Effectiveness</span>: Each GTR should possess strong individual capabilities, contributing significantly to the overall QA process. This ensures that each component of the set is valuable and enhances the QA performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following these refined principles, we construct an instance of the zero-shot GTR pool <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{ZS}=\{V_{dot},V_{neato},V_{circo},V_{fdp},V_{sfdp},T_{set},T_{list},T_{mat}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{ZS}=\{V_{dot},V_{neato},V_{circo},V_{fdp},V_{sfdp},T_{set},T_{list},T_{mat}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of the following GTRs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual GTRs (5 types):</span> The GTRs <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{dot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dot}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{neato}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{neato}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{circo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{circo}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{fdp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{fdp}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{sfdp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{sfdp}</annotation></semantics></math> are generated based on methods from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> using different layout algorithms provided by Graphviz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Specifically, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{dot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>dot</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{dot}}</annotation></semantics></math> arranges nodes in tree-like hierarchical layers; <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{neato}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>neato</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{neato}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses the spring model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to minimize edge crossings on canvas; <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{circo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>circo</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{circo}}</annotation></semantics></math> positions nodes in a circular pattern; <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{fdp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>fdp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{fdp}}</annotation></semantics></math> offers a fast force-directed layout with optimized computational overhead; and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{sfdp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>sfdp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{sfdp}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a scalable force-directed layout for efficiently handling large graphs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Textual GTRs (3 types):</span> These GTRs <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{set}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{set}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{list}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{list}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{mat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{mat}</annotation></semantics></math> present the graph topology structure via Edge set, Adjacent List, and Adjacent Matrix, respectively. The prompt templates of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{set}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{set}</annotation></semantics></math> are retrieved from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, and the others are designed by us.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the GTRs generation details in Appendix A.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21864v1/figures/DGTR_GTR_6.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:309/280;" width="309" height="280" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An Illustration of eight candidate GTRs in <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{ZS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{ZS}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Constructing the 𝑅_{𝑍⁢𝑆} ‣ 3 Zero-shot GTR Pool ‣ DynamicGTR: Leveraging Graph Topology Representation Preferences to Boost VLM Capabilities on Graph QAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the examples of GTRs in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{ZS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{ZS}</annotation></semantics></math>, all of which are <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model-agnostic</span> generated before input to VLMs, ensuring their usability with closed-source models. Visual GTRs enable rapid, intuitive perception of topology, while textual GTRs offer slower, analytical understanding, mirroring dual-system cognitive frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> further show that layouts and prompt templates substantially influence graph QA performance, supporting our emphasis on <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Variety</span>. Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DynamicGTR: Leveraging Graph Topology Representation Preferences to Boost VLM Capabilities on Graph QAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> empirically validates the <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">effectiveness</span> of each GTR across tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21864v1/figures/DGTR_framework_2.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/281;" width="685" height="281" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the DynamicGTR framework, where the GTR Router guides the VLM Reasoner to use the most appropriate GTR based on the question.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>DynamicGTR</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the DynamicGTR framework, which enhances the zero-shot graph QA of VLMs by dynamic GTR routing, on both accuracy and brevity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VLM-based zero-shot graph QA leverages pretrained VLMs to tackle a wide range of graph problems, without access to or modification of the VLM specifics. This approach facilitates effective generalization across various tasks and unseen graph data, while fully preserving the inherent capabilities of VLMs across the other universal domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Framework Overview</h3>

