<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effects on
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Effects on 𝑘. ‣ 5.4 Hyperparameter Effects Analysis ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DynamicGTR: Leveraging Graph Topology Representation Preferences to Boost VLM Capabilities on Graph QAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the impact of the hyperparameter <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. By repeating inferences across trials, increasing
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> improves the quality of the GTR preference dataset by aligning probing results more closely with the true GTR priority distribution, resulting in more accurate and concise responses. Even with limited resources, using a small
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> still achieves remarkable gains compared to the best baseline.</p>
</div>
