<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">E. Results for LLaVA-OneVision-7B and Qwen3-VL-8B</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An advantage of the DynamicGTR framework is its generally usability on closed-source VLMs, because it does not require any access to model parameter or architecture details, but that not means DynamicGTR cannot run on open-source VLMs. We have shown its significant effectiveness on leading closed-source VLMs including GPT-4o and Gemini-2.5-Pro in the main body of the manuscript. Here in this supplementary material section, we supplement results on the leading open-source VLMs, including LLaVA-OneVision-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and Qwen3-VL-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> in Table <a href="#Sx5.T12" title="Table L ‣ E. Results for LLaVA-OneVision-7B and Qwen3-VL-8B ‣ DynamicGTR: Leveraging Graph Topology Representation Preferences to Boost VLM Capabilities on Graph QAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a> and <a href="#Sx5.T13" title="Table M ‣ E. Results for LLaVA-OneVision-7B and Qwen3-VL-8B ‣ DynamicGTR: Leveraging Graph Topology Representation Preferences to Boost VLM Capabilities on Graph QAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>.</p>
</div>
